\title{The Metaplectic Extension of the Gabor Window Action}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{6 September 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
The Gabor (windowed Fourier) transform is built from two operators, translation and modulation,
which generate the Heisenberg group $\Heis$ through the Weyl cocycle $\chi(ba')$, where
$\chi(x)=e^{2\pi ix}$. We adjoin a third generator, the \emph{chirp} $(C_cf)(t)=e^{2\pi ict^2}f(t)$,
and prove that the Weyl cocycle extends: $C_c$ normalises $\Heis$ via the shear automorphism
$\sigma_c(a,b,z)=(a,b+2ca,z\chi(ca^2))$, so that $\Heis$ is a normal subgroup of a semidirect
product $\Heis\rtimes\RR$ carrying a faithful representation on window space. We then identify the
geometry: the chirped Gaussians $G_{\alpha,\beta}(t)=e^{-\pi(\alpha+i\beta)t^2}$, $\alpha>0$, form a
family parametrised by the upper half-plane through the Siegel parameter $z=i/(\alpha+i\beta)$, and
chirp, dilation and Fourier transform act on $z$ by the M\"obius transformations of the shear,
diagonal and rotation-by-$\pi/2$ subgroups of $\SL$; in particular
$\widehat{G_\tau}=\tau^{-1/2}G_{1/\tau}$ for $\Rea\tau>0$. Consequences: the classical width
parameter is the coordinate $z=is^2$ on the imaginary geodesic, so scale-space monotonicity is the
diagonal flow; the lower Borel subgroup acts transitively on the family and lifts to window
operators without any sign; and the chirp direction is transverse to the width geodesic. Finally we
isolate the obstruction. The central element $S^2=-I$ acts trivially on the upper half-plane, yet
$\Ft^2$ is the parity operator, $\Ft^2(g_{s,a,b})=g_{s,-a,-b}$, which differs from the identity on
every Gabor atom with $(a,b)\ne(0,0)$, while $\Ft^4=\mathrm{id}$. The obstruction is moreover
projective: no complex rescaling $\kappa\Ft^2$ reproduces the atom. Hence the phase-space action
does not lift to an action on windows --- only the metaplectic double cover acts, and the
discrepancy is a genuine order-two cocycle class supported at the Weyl element. Discretely, the
shear preserves the integer Heisenberg lattice if and only if $2c\in\ZZ$.
\end{abstract}

